  "version": 1,
  "comments": {
    "c1": {
      "anchor": "t41",
      "kind": "item",
      "label": "Follow up: P26: a library's own new thing is made by name only for the types its project file exports",
      "excerpt": "Follow up: P26: a library's own new thing is made by name only for the types its project file exports",
      "text": "clarify",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-07T22:12:14.764Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-07T22:13:39.767Z",
          "html": "<p>What T41 means, in plain words:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>\n    A library (say, a cards library) can make things of its own kinds: its deck makes cards with <code>a new card</code>.\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    When Solitaire uses that library, you'd want the deck to make <em>Solitaire's</em> card (with Solitaire's own looks and\n    rules), not the library's plain one.\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    Since P26 that happens, but only when the library's project file lists the type in its <code>exports</code>:\n    <code>\"exports\": [\"Card\", \"Deck\"]</code>. Without that line, the deck keeps making the library's own plain cards.\n  </li>\n  <li>\n    I chose that so no existing project's compiled code changed. Making it the rule for every type would have changed 5\n    files in recipe-box, recetario, live-room and sala-en-vivo, for no gain.\n  </li>\n</ul>\n<p>The follow-up question T41 holds: is \"only what <code>exports</code> lists\" the rule you want, or should a library always let the project using it supply its own version of each type?</p>\n<epic-net-effect><ul><li>Nothing changes unless you pick the other rule;  then every library type is made by name, and those 5 files' compiled code changes.</li></ul></epic-net-effect>"
        }
      ]
    },
    "c2": {
      "anchor": "j68",
      "kind": "item",
      "label": "P45: a plain page is read by the reader that read its tag, so P47 keeps that reader's page half",
      "excerpt": "P45: a plain page is read by the reader that read its tag, so P47 keeps that reader's page half",
      "text": "Would writing a new reader clean up anything?",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-11T13:21:52.947Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-11T13:38:29.872Z",
          "html": "<p>A little, not much.  Most of the page reader is what a page's body needs, and a new reader would have to write all of that again.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>What it would remove:\n    <ul>\n      <li>a page's own \"form\":  its own tokenizer and its own parser beside each project's, checked in about 10 places (the parser's 6, the line rule, the drawing rule, the file, the editor's colouring, the language server)</li>\n      <li>the head read as a tag, then twinned back into <code>x is a page</code> + <code>to draw it</code>, and the scan that reads a page file before it parses (<code>skeletonOf()</code>):  about 150 lines</li>\n      <li>two ways to read <code>&lt;if&gt;</code>:  on a page's own lines it's a page tag, twinned into an <code>if</code> line;  inside an element it's the base tokenizer's chain tag.  And a plain file's <code>&lt;for&gt;</code> inside an element is re-read through a page parser.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li>What it would NOT remove:  everything plain Spell doesn't have.\n    <ul>\n      <li>prose lines drawn as markdown, <code>##</code> sections, <code>&lt;!-- --&gt;</code> comments, fenced code</li>\n      <li><code>&lt;spell&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> blocks, <code>&lt;/&gt;</code> closers checked against their tag</li>\n      <li><code>&lt;for&gt;</code>'s riders (loading, empty, failed, paging), and every message in English and Spanish</li>\n      <li>That's most of today's 1,730 lines (tokenizer 898, twin 628, tokens 177, closer 24).  A new reader would rewrite it, not delete it.  My guess:  300 to 600 lines fewer in the end, nearly all from the form and twin plumbing.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li>What it costs:  about a phase.  All 387 pages (every Spell UI notebook) must compile the same, byte for byte;  the editor's colouring and the language server read the page tags and the twin's words, so they change too;  Spanish too.</li>\n  <li>What it risks:\n    <ul>\n      <li>plain Spell's tokenizer reads every file:  page rules there cost every plain file (the parser speed test would say how much)</li>\n      <li>a notebook page that draws a little differently, which nobody notices</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li>My recommendation:  keep A.  If you want the cleanup, take the cheap part as its own item:  ONE way to read <code>&lt;if&gt;</code> / <code>&lt;otherwise&gt;</code> / <code>&lt;for&gt;</code> in a drawing, so a plain file stops borrowing a page parser for <code>&lt;for&gt;</code>.</li>\n</ul>"
        }
      ],
      "done": {
        "how": "skip",
        "at": "2026-11-11T13:55:11.720Z",
        "by": "owenoak"
      },
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-11T13:37:40.716Z"
      }
    },
    "c3": {
      "anchor": "i74",
      "kind": "item",
      "label": "Six of your own projects in spell.spaces are still tag files, and won't read once this branch merges",
      "excerpt": "Six of your own projects in spell.spaces are still tag files, and won't read once this branch merges",
      "text": "Merge first, then fix",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-11T13:22:57.598Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-11T13:38:49.895Z",
          "html": "<p>Converted all six, now <code>.spell</code> in the new forms (no colon before a body, a one-line comment inside its declaration).  Each project file's import now names the <code>.spell</code> file.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Each one compiles with the current kit with no errors.  The four <code>@willzalone</code> apps also compile to the same program as the library's Inbox, OrgAdmin, OrgChat and OrgFeed;  only the org's name in comments differs.</li>\n  <li>Their <code>.api.spell</code> files were brought to the new forms too, with no <code>- </code> bullets and no colons.  They still read as they were.</li>\n  <li><b>Not converted:  59 more <code>.spellml</code> files in <code>~/www/jarvis</code></b>, which <code>owen@/jarvis-current</code> and <code>jarvis-new</code> link to.  They don't read any more either.</li>\n</ul>"
        },
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "at": "2026-11-11T13:54:05.239Z",
          "text": "fix jarvis too, no remnants in the old form anywhere",
          "edited": "2026-11-11T13:56:24.135Z"
        },
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-11T14:04:45.943Z",
          "html": "<p>jarvis is done too, and I swept everywhere else.  No file in the tag form is left in anything you use.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>jarvis:  all 59 files are now <code>.spell</code> (1 in <code>current/</code>, 58 in <code>new/</code>), and both project files name them.\n    <ul>\n      <li>Both sites compile with the current kit.  The only message is a warning the original had too:  <code>theme is always \"mockup\"</code>.</li>\n      <li>Every site line still reads in the site editor, with each card's row, column, colour, thumbnail and live link kept.</li>\n      <li><b>One thing has no plain form:  a group's colour</b> (<code>&lt;group \"Home\" color=\"red\"/&gt;</code>), and one group's description (\"Baton\").  They're kept as a comment at the top of <code>JarvisNew.site.spell</code>.</li>\n      <li>Left for you, not committed.  The diff also holds your own uncommitted edits from Nov 9 to the same files.</li>\n      <li><code>tools/make-sites.mjs</code> still writes the tag form.  It's the one-time generator, so I left it as it is.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li>coming-soon:  its Solitaire and Solitario were still the tag form (8 files).  I built them again with the current kit (<code>spell app preview viewer</code>, the command its last commit names).  The game draws in a headless browser.  Not committed.</li>\n  <li>The kit:  4 places fixed in the language branch (commit 85af7cc4e):\n    <ul>\n      <li>app's README Task example and parser's README Carta example, in plain Spell</li>\n      <li>the calculator and tic-tac-toe guides' comments, which still taught <code>&lt;property&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;action&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;thing&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;alias&gt;</code></li>\n      <li>the card-example draft</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li>Left as they are:  <code>~/www/spell.old</code> (an archive of the old repos, 39 files), the epic repo's Spellx guide (its subject is the tag form, so retiring it is your call), and code comments and tests that quote the tag form (listed in my report).</li>\n  <li>Backups:  <code>~/.spell/backups/language-i74/</code>.</li>\n</ul>"
        },
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "at": "2026-11-11T14:11:30.336Z",
          "text": "add color to group"
        }
